## Changes

### 1. `src/utils/date-time-aria-labels.ts`

Extract a shared helper to avoid duplicating `getWeekInfo()` logic:

```typescript
// Add above getDayNames()
export function getLocaleFirstDay(locale = 'en'): number {
  const localeInfo = new Intl.Locale(locale);
  // @ts-expect-error - getWeekInfo is not typed
  return localeInfo.getWeekInfo().firstDay % 7; // 0=Sun, 1=Mon … 6=Sat
}
```

Refactor `getDayNames()` to use it (removes duplicated `getWeekInfo` call):
```typescript
const firstDayOfWeek = getLocaleFirstDay(locale); // was: localeInfo.getWeekInfo().firstDay
DAY_NAMES.set(cacheKey, days.slice(firstDayOfWeek).concat(days.slice(0, firstDayOfWeek)));
// Note: slice index changes because getLocaleFirstDay returns 0-based JS index
//       (old: slice(firstDayOfWeek - 1); new: slice(firstDayOfWeek))
```

### 2. `src/elements/use-calendar/use-calendar.ts`

**Import `getLocaleFirstDay`:**
```typescript
import { getDayNames, getLocaleFirstDay } from '../../utils/date-time-aria-labels.js';
```

**Rename `#firstDayOfWeek` → `#startDayOffset`** and make locale-aware:
```typescript
// BEFORE (line 221-223):
get #firstDayOfWeek() {
  return new Date(this.year, this.month - 1, 1).getDay();
}

// AFTER:
get #startDayOffset() {
  const firstOfMonth = new Date(this.year, this.month - 1, 1);
  return (firstOfMonth.getDay() - getLocaleFirstDay(this.locale) + 7) % 7;
}
```

**Update `render()` (line 574)** — fix startDate branch to also be locale-aware:
```typescript
// BEFORE:
const firstDay = startDate && this.#isThisMonth(startDate) ? startDate.getDay() : this.#firstDayOfWeek;

// AFTER:
const localeFirstDay = getLocaleFirstDay(this.locale);
const firstDay = startDate && this.#isThisMonth(startDate)
  ? (startDate.getDay() - localeFirstDay + 7) % 7
  : this.#startDayOffset;
```

**Update prev-month day formula (line 584)** — simplify to use `firstDay` directly:
```typescript
// BEFORE:
previousMonthData.days - previousMonthData.firstDay + i - 1

// AFTER:
previousMonthData.days - firstDay + i
```
(The `firstDay` in the new code is the column offset, making the old two-variable formula
unnecessary. Math verified: for March 2023 with Monday-first locale, both approaches yield
identical prev-month dates.)

